@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Data Access";
}
@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}
<div class="mdi-action-info help" title="More information">
    <ul class="info">
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span> Guidelines that you should never skip and should be applicable to all situations.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span> Strongly recommended guidelines.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span> Recommended guidelines that may not be applicable in all situations.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW2501 -</span> Use <i>Fanex.Data</i> in data access. <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p><i>Fanex.Data</i> supports many utilities for dealing with user stored procedure.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Query a result set</li>
                <li>Tool supports generating criteria and entities automatically</li>
                <li>Mapping <i>IEnumerable</i> with result set</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW2502 -</span> Encrypt password for SQL server accounts. <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                Password in plain text violates security policy. It should be encrypted by DNAide<br />
                For examples: Uqeq4MmLwveHZzqok3kHGf7fqd4SEfMq0vtQ5l2TQ50==
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW2503 -</span> Use <i>ExecuteNonQuery</i> for store procedures which do not return values. <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                You can use the <i>ExecuteNonQuery</i> to perform catalog operations 
                (for example, querying the structure of a database or creating database objects such as tables), 
                or to change the data in a database without using a <i>DataSet</i> by executing <i>UPDATE</i>, <i>INSERT</i>, or <i>DELETE</i> statements.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW2504 -</span> Avoid using <i>DataSet</i>, using <i>Entity</i> instead.  <span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span></h2>
            <p>
                A <i>DataSet</i> is (to quote Microsoft) an in-memory cache of data. 
                Essentially it’s a snapshot of part of your database. <i>DataSets</i> can be very useful, 
                but they have some significant annoyances too: performance, memory, code maintainability. 
            </p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public virtual IEnumerable&lt;customerwinloss&gt; GetCustomerWinLoss(ICriteria criteria)
                    {
                        Check.Argument.IsNotNull(criteria, "criteria");
                        Check.Argument.IsValid(criteria, "criteria");          
                        return _dynamicRepository.Fetch&lt;customerwinloss&gt;(criteria);
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW2505 -</span> Specify timeout value in data access requests.  <span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span></h2>
            <p>The timeout value should be set to reduce the overhead of database.</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    using (var objectDb = new ObjectDb(criteria.GetSettingKey(), 120))
                    {
                        var param = criteria.GetSpParams();
                        objectDb.ExecuteNonQuery(param);
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW2506 -</span> Use criteria to pass parameters to stored procedure.  <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                <i>Fanex.Data</i> supports passing Criteria to stored procedure. It will help our code be more readable and maintainable. 
            </p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    using (var objectDb = new ObjectDb(criteria.GetSettingKey(), 120))
                    {
                       var param = criteria.GetSpParams();
                        objectDb.ExecuteNonQuery(param);
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="scroll-top">
    <span class="mdi-editor-publish icon-scroll-top"></span>
</div>
